Z||000016||stimpy||08-09-2000||06:41 PM||||The idea I have batted about in my head would be to fabricate an air intake that goes across the bottom of the firewall. This would take advantage of the vacuum effect of the low pressure area behind the windows that sucks the air through the engine compartment. I don't undstand the logic of running air the entire length of the car, it's not like a vacuum exists underneath the car. Furthermore, I would think that the air would compress as it passed underneath the car.  I would think that a fairly massive airbox coud be constructd that would barely drop below the bottom line of the car.  <BR>I can't be the only one who has thought of this...<P>PS spiro dont make the mistake of being offended by Patrick thats just his sense of humor and im sure he desnt mean anything by it just let it slide ok buddy its all good<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Old Lar||08-09-2000||08:00 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/CMf_1.jpg"> <P>The scoops did not draw in air to cool the Northstar engine that was in the back.  They may do some venting of the radiator, but mostly for good looks.  And they did look good.  I don't know Chris's secrets in the construction of this car, but he did a fantastic job!<p>[This message has been edited by Old Lar (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Pontiaddict||08-09-2000||08:19 PM||||I wonder if it's possible to use that ram air hood to force air over some hood vents causing cause a venturi (sp?) vacuum effect like on an indy car. (Though not as good)<P>Wondering this for a while and even did some planning, but my fiberglass skills aren't very good. Might still do it though. I figure  I'd put the idea out there and if someone wanted to try, go for it.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Philphine||08-10-2000||02:42 AM||||why not just get an indy scoop. i guess pontiac did some research to make it perform well on the real pace cars. i have done my own very precise scientific research (ok, i put my fingers up through the sunroof and felt a pretty good rush of air that would be going into my scoop). if nothing else it should be a good sourse of cooler outside air.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000020||WKDFIRO||08-10-2000||03:22 AM||||Re: Fierocious<P>I would disagree that the ram air scoops are completely useless (cosmetics aside.) The 10 inches distance from the car (the air "wake") is not a COMPLETE vacuum. I do agree that holley type scoops are is no where near as efficient as the naca ducts used on the front of the Viper. I had thought I had felt a very small difference when I put on the scoop but I remembered I had replaced the water trap thing with a straight tube. <P>Inconclusive evidence at best. <P>    When it comes down to it, taking out the vent and replacing it with the scoop can't be absolutly useless in aiding intake velocities. It just may not make any extraordinary strides in hp or torque. As long as it isn't hurting performance, it can't be all bad.<P>Thus concludes my 2 cents! Thank you!<P>Cha-chunk. ||||reg||5
Z||000021||HugGRRRFiero||08-10-2000||09:12 AM||||   I haven't taken Fluid dynamics yet, couple more terms to go before that one, so I'm no expert on the subject.  I think the effect of running a ram air tube from the front of the car to the rear into the the intake would be about the same effect as the engine sucking air from a long straw.  The tube diameter would have to be so large that it wouldn't be practical.<P>   As for vent scoops, I've got one of our BBJ Performance side air scoops from  <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bbjproducts/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bbjproducts/</A>  bolted on mine.  It has about a 3" dia. opening that sticks out just enough so if you we're to look directly along the side of the car from the front you can just see the opening protrude.  I don't have dyno tests to prove anything, but I think it maybe helps add 5 or so HP.  Anyway, for me it looks functional and cool!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif"> <BR> <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Formula||08-10-2000||10:26 AM||||what if you put the scoop on the back and drove backwards all the time?||||reg||1
Z||000023||Pontiaddict||08-10-2000||04:47 PM||||I wouldn't use the Indy scoop simply because it blocks what is left of any rearward visibility on the car.<P>How about adding a NACA duct to the sail panels. The scoops in the sail panels on my GT (those fasback aftermarket replacements) seem to duct air over the decklid louvers.<BR>Don't the ducts in the side windows of a NASCAR stock car disprove the slipstream theory? <P>Formula, if you were driving around backwards all the time, the whole back end of the car would be a scoop.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Monkeyman||08-11-2000||01:38 AM||||The NACA ducts in the side windows of NASCAR cars are for fresh air to the driver.  They have nothing to do with the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000025||FIEROCIOUS||08-11-2000||06:45 PM||||Monkeyman, I was describeing the shape of a naca duct I didn't suggest they were for the engines use on nascar cars. Naca ducts have been used on cars for years to support fresh air for helmets, cool brakes, intake air, or even battery venting.<P>The naca duct actualy supports the slipstream effect. The thiner begining of the duct is a high pressure area the wider later part of the duct is a low pressure area. Air ALWAYS moves from a high pressure area to a low pressure area due to physics. <P>I think some of you are not understanding that the slipstream is not completely void of air. That would require a sealed vacume!<BR>Yes some air flows along the car surface but it is turbulent and minimal compaired to about 8-10 inches away from the car. <P>Did you ever wonder why dragsters have huge scoops 10-12 above the hood?<P>It's not so it looks cool!||||reg||1
Z||000026||Cooter||08-11-2000||08:14 PM||||Pontiaddict, I have the Indy scoop and visibility is not affected as much as you might think. It takes a little getting used to is all.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Philphine||08-12-2000||02:30 AM||||i like my indy scoop too. plan to get another for my indy.||||reg||1
Z||000028||86SE||08-12-2000||04:50 AM||||I think the under-the-car thing would work.<P>If you have the aero package you could make  1 scoop with a wide mouth that covers 1 side of the 2 openings.  Then duct it to the rear and come up through the left qtr panel to the intake.<P>Drive 100 MPH and get a good wind built up, you would have a couple lbs of boost..... <P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>**Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention**||||reg||1
