Z||000034||SCCA FIERO||08-14-2000||02:11 AM||||On my 88 there is a rubber flap about a foot long attatched to the cradle just below the muffler. I think it's there to "scoop" air up into the engine bay and the up through the deck vents.<P>As far as the Holley scoop goes... I don't have actual proof that it "rams" air, but it does make it so my air filter needs to be cleaned more often than without it. Also at about 60 mph, it "feels" like there is a bit more umph than without it. That could be in my head though.<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe<BR><B>KEEP THE EXCITEMENT ALIVE!!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000035||BroCar 87||08-14-2000||06:42 PM||||Alrighty GUYS, I got another question! If i did have a ram air type of deal on my car. how would it be measured on a dyno. Doesn't there have to be a force of air being pushed in it at the opening for it to be RAMMED? is there a blower or something. HOW WOULD IT SHOW ANY DIFFERENCE IN HAVING THE RAM AIR AND NOT HAVING THE RAM AIR?<P>OHH by the way, I am going to devise up something. I just haven't decided where from. Under the car being filtered sounds GOOD. BUT i do see the risks of making it there. <BR>There are pro's and cons of both places. I will let you know what i come up with though.<P>THANKS<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NorthFLFieroSIG.jpg">  <BR>87 Coupe "IRON BEAST"<BR>84 SE (project)||||reg||6
